    "result_text": "The build is green. \n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented per-country national-number length validation for E.164 telephone numbers in the owner-creation flow.\n\n**Change** \u2014 `OwnerRestControllerV1.java`, in the telephone normalization path:\n- The `toE164(...)` method already normalized numbers to E.164 form and enforced a general 8\u201315 digit rule. I added a call to a new `validateNationalLength(...)` helper after that check.\n- `validateNationalLength(...)` compares the national number (digits after the country code) against a required length per country code: `+61` requires 9 national digits, `+1` requires 10. A mismatch throws `InvalidTelephoneException`, which the existing `ExceptionControllerAdvice` maps to a **400 Bad Request**. Country codes without a configured requirement fall through to the general rule, preserving prior behavior.\n- Updated the javadoc on both `toE164` and `InvalidTelephoneException` to describe the new rule.\n\nThe lengths are held in a small `Map<String,Integer>` (`\"61\"\u21929`, `\"1\"\u219210`), keeping it easy to extend. The two codes don't overlap as prefixes, so iteration order is irrelevant.\n\n**Verification** \u2014 `./mvnw -o test`: `Tests run: 1, Failures: 0, Errors: 0` / `BUILD SUCCESS`. The new acceptance test `errorRejectsWrongNationalLength` (`+61 123` \u2192 400, since `+61` needs 9 national digits) passes, and the valid `uniqueTelephone()` payload (`04XXXXXXXX` \u2192 `+61` + 9 national digits) still succeeds across the rule chain.",
